**assistant**:
> Most likely: your child is a **candidate**, and `poolprice` deliberately returns pooled draws from **validated models only**:
> 
> ```julia
> validated = filter(m -> m["status"] == "validated", ms)
> ```
> 
> So it can successfully run the child, write its individual prediction into `predictions/`, yet return:
> 
> ```julia
> Dict{String,Vector{Float64}}()
> ```
> 
> until at least one model is validated.
> 
> Inspect this:
> 
> ```julia
> PoolPredict.generatediagnostics()
> println(read(joinpath(PoolPredict.ROOT, "diagnostics.toml"), String))
> ```
> 
> Or directly:
> 
> ```julia
> [(m["id"], m["status"]) for m in PoolPredict.models()]
> ```
> 
> Also inspect the newest prediction snapshot’s errors and individual outputs:
> 
> ```julia
> snap = PoolPredict.snapshots()[end]
> snap["errors"]
> keys(snap["individual"])
> ```
> 
> If your child ID appears in `snap["individual"]`, it ran correctly; it simply was not eligible for pooling.
> 
> For initial testing, promote it manually:
> 
> ```julia
> s = PoolPredict.state()
> for m in s["models"]
>     if m["id"] == childid
>         m["status"] = "validated"
>     end
> end
> PoolPredict.savestate(s)
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```julia
> prepared = PoolPredict.prepare_pool_context()
> PoolPredict.poolprice(1, prepared)
> ```
> 
> Longer-term, you need one or more initial “seed” models marked `"validated"` when added, because candidates require at least `min_candidate_events` resolved outcomes before `manage_pool()` can promote them. With the current default of 20, that means they will not become eligible immediately.
